{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>And her maidens walking, etc.</b> One must examine the intent of this narrative — <b>and her maidens walking</b>. Perhaps it intends to tell of the deed of Batyah, for she performed a great mitzvah, and the verse was precise to inform us of the details of how the deed was done. Now it is a well-known thing that a king's daughter does not go to the river without maidens, as is the custom of princesses. Also, from the conduct of great personages and especially kings, it is customary for them to have one designated, highly honored attendant to be present before them and serve them and bathe in the bathhouse or in the river and the like. Here the verse informed us, by saying ״וְנַעֲרוֹתֶיהָ הוֹלְכוֹת,״ that it was at the time she was bathing, when her maidens were required by royal protocol not to stand before her, and none remained with her except one designated attendant — and she sent her, as it says, <b>and she sent her maidservant</b>, meaning the one designated for her to bathe, etc. And His saying <b>and she took her</b> is balanced — either she sent her away so that she would not advise her contrary to her will, for Batyah's will was to save that child, and Batyah took the ark; or <b>and she sent</b> etc. <b>and her maidservant took her</b> — and for both ways it declares the praise of Batyah, that her soul was a pure soul: מִי יִתֵּן טָהוֹר מִטָּמֵא (Job 14:4). For the first way it intends to say that even though only one maidservant was with her and all her maidens were walking, etc., nevertheless she feared being prevented by her, and sent her away, and the king's daughter remained alone and took the ark with her own hand. And for the second way, too, it declares praise: even though only one maidservant was with her, and it is not according to proper conduct for a king's daughter to remain alone — and needless to say at the bank of the river — nevertheless she sent her far away. Therefore it prefaced by informing us <b>and her maidens</b> etc., in order to understand the intent of the statement <b>and she sent her maidservant</b> in the manner we have written; and had it not said ״וְנַעֲרוֹתֶיהָ,״ the intention would not have been known to be what we explained. Understand this."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:23:23.880356853Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}